  "Number": 7,
  "Question": "A carton contains milk that is \\(2\\%\\) fat, an amount that is \\(40\\%\\) less fat than the amount contained in a carton of whole milk. What is the percentage of fat in whole milk?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{12}{5}\\)",
    "B": "\\(3\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{10}{3}\\)",
    "D": "\\(38\\)",
    "E": "\\(42\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "A \\(40\\%\\) reduction leaves \\(100\\%-40\\%=60\\%\\) of the original fat level. Thus the \\(2\\%\\) fat level is \\(60\\%=\\frac35\\) of the whole-milk fat level.\n\nTo recover the original level, divide by the fraction that remains:\n\\[\n2\\%\\div\\frac35\n=2\\%\\cdot\\frac53\n=\\frac{10}{3}\\%.\n\\]\nWhole milk is therefore \\(\\boxed{\\frac{10}{3}\\%}\\) fat, choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
